            "content": "## Problem\n\nPinning is a statement about **importance**. Ordering pinned items by **recency** contradicts it.\n\nToday every chat list — including the pinned one — is ordered the same way. `ChatsRepository.findByOwner` ends in a single `.orderBy(desc(chats.updatedAt))` for all three pin filter modes (`only` / `with` / `exclude`), so `GET /chats?pinned=only` returns pinned chats in last-touched order. A chat pinned precisely because it is a long-running reference sinks below one that got a throwaway reply five minutes ago, and the user has no way to stop that. The only thing pinning currently buys is membership in a separate list, not a position within it.\n\nThe substrate is already half-built: `pins` carries `pinned_at` and indexes it (`pins_user_pinned_idx` on `(user_id, pinned_at DESC, item_id)`, migration `0023`), but nothing orders by it — and pin *time* is only a marginally better proxy for importance than update time. Neither is the user's actual intent, which is an explicit sequence.\n\n## Outcome\n\nA user-defined explicit order over their own pinned items, persisted per user, honored by every surface that lists pins — the pinned chat list, the pinned projects list, and any future consumer — with a documented, stable rule for where a newly pinned item lands and how existing rows are ordered on migration.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **An explicit rank on `pins`.** The table is already keyed `(user_id, item_type, item_id)` and covers both `'chat'` and `'project'` (`pin_item_type`), so ordering belongs here rather than on `chats`. Decide and document whether rank is scoped per `item_type` (chats and projects ordered independently, matching how they are listed today) or across the whole pin set — the former is expected, since the two are never rendered in one list.\n- **Choose the rank representation deliberately; it is expensive to change.** A dense integer position requires rewriting every row after an insert; a sparse/fractional key (lexicographic rank, gap-based integers) makes a reorder a single-row write at the cost of periodic rebalancing. Reordering is a per-user operation over a small set, so both are viable — pick on write-amplification and concurrency, not on aesthetics, and state the choice.\n- **Backfill is part of the contract, not an afterthought.** Existing pins must receive a deterministic initial order. `pinned_at DESC` is the natural seed (it is what the user's pin sequence actually was) and it is already indexed. Ordering by `updated_at` at migration time would bake today's accidental order in permanently.\n- **Where a newly pinned item lands.** Top is the expected answer — pinning is usually \"I need this now\" — but it must be stated, because it determines whether the rank space grows at the head or the tail.\n- **API surface.** Reorder is a partial update of the pin resource, not an RPC verb handle, per the RESTful-resource convention in `apps/api/AGENTS.md`. It needs a DTO, an explicit response type with an egress allowlist, and an OpenAPI entry. Reordering another user's pins must be impossible: identity comes from the session, never from request input, and the existing `pins` row-level-security policy is the datastore backstop.\n- **Every listing consumer moves together.** `findByOwner`'s `pinned='only'` mode is the current one; the web pinned category consumes it. A consumer left on `updated_at` ordering silently disagrees with the user's arrangement.\n- **`pinned='exclude'` ordering is unchanged.** The unpinned list stays recency-ordered; only the pinned list gains explicit order.\n- UI (drag-to-reorder or otherwise) is a separate concern and is not specified here.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] A user can set an explicit order over their pinned chats, and `GET /chats?pinned=only` returns them in that order.\n- [ ] The same holds for pinned projects.\n- [ ] One user's ordering never affects another user's, proven in the row-level-security integration suite.\n- [ ] A reorder request naming another user's pin is rejected or ignored; the target's order is unchanged.\n- [ ] Existing pins are ordered deterministically after migration, seeded from `pinned_at DESC`, with no user losing pin membership.\n- [ ] Pinning a new item places it at the documented position without renumbering breaking any other consumer.\n- [ ] `pinned='exclude'` results are byte-identical to today.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nNo manual ordering of unpinned chats — recency is correct there, and an explicit order over an unbounded list is a different feature. No cross-user or shared ordering. No folders, no nesting, no drag-and-drop implementation (UI is out of scope). No change to what pinning *means* for archival, visibility, or access.\n\n## Cross-links\n\n- **#307 (recency digest) consumes this.** The digest renders a capped list of the owner's pinned chats, and it was specified against today's `updated_at DESC` ordering explicitly because manual ordering did not exist. When this ships, the digest's pinned section must follow the user's order — the whole point of surfacing pinned chats to the model is that the user said they matter, and the user's own sequence is the best available statement of *how much*. The digest's spec should reference this rather than re-deriving an ordering.\n- **#327 (`recent_chats` tool)** takes the same treatment if it surfaces or flags pinned chats, for the same reason: the tool and the digest must not disagree about order.\n- `pins` and its row-level-security model shipped in migration `0023` (rework-item-pinning), which replaced row-level `chats.pinned_at` with the per-user `pins` table.\n",
